binet-by-the-door">17. Mount a slim shoe cabinet by the door</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#18-choose-hooks-over-a-coat-rack">18. Choose hooks over a coat rack</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#keep-it-working">Keep It Working</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#19-use-clear-bins-and-labels">19. Use clear bins and labels</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#20-follow-a-one-in-one-out-rule">20. Follow a one in, one out rule</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#common-mistakes-to-avoid">Common Mistakes to Avoid</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#faq">FAQ</a><ul class="kb-table-of-contents-list-sub"><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-do-i-maximize-storage-in-a-small-space">How do I maximize storage in a small space?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#what-are-the-best-storage-solutions-for-small-spaces-when-renting">What are the best storage solutions for small spaces when renting?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#how-can-i-make-a-small-room-look-less-cluttered">How can I make a small room look less cluttered?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#where-should-i-store-things-in-a-small-apartment-with-no-closet">Where should I store things in a small apartment with no closet?</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#final-thoughts">Final Thoughts</a></li></ul></div></nav>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Think in Zones, Not Square Feet</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Small homes rarely run out of space. They run out of <em>floor</em> space. A typical wall has five storage zones, and most of us only use the middle one. That&#8217;s why the best storage ideas for small spaces start with the wall, not the floor. Put the things you use every day where they&#8217;re easiest to reach, and send everything else higher, lower or under furniture.</p>



<figure class="dsh-diagram" style="margin:2em 0;"><svg viewBox="0 0 1200 900" width="100%" role="img" aria-labelledby="dssm-t dssm-d" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="font-family:Poppins,'Helvetica Neue',Arial,sans-serif;height:auto;display:block;border-radius:12px;"><title id="dssm-t">Five storage zones on one wall of a small space</title><desc id="dssm-d">A wall in a small room divided into five storage zones. Zone 1, above doors at 84 inches (213 cm) and higher: seasonal items and spare bedding. Zone 2, high shelves from 60 to 84 inches (150 to 213 cm): light items used weekly. Zone 3, hip to eye level from 24 to 60 inches (60 to 150 cm): things used daily, the easiest zone to reach. Zone 4, low shelves under 24 inches (60 cm): heavy items and kids&#8217; things. Zone 5, under furniture such as beds, benches and sofas: flat bins and off-season items. Tip: keep heavy things low and anchor tall shelves, as recommended by the CPSC.</desc><rect width="1200" height="900" fill="#F7F1E9"/><text x="60" y="84" font-size="44" font-weight="700" fill="#342E2A">Use the Whole Wall: 5 Storage Zones</text><text x="60" y="124" font-size="24" fill="#6E645C">store things by how often you use them</text><text x="1140" y="124" font-size="20" fill="#C85A32" text-anchor="end">dailyspacehacks.com</text><rect x="60" y="160" width="540" height="560" rx="14" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#D8CBBB" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="62" y="162" width="536" height="86" fill="#ECCDA0" opacity="0.3"/><rect x="62" y="374" width="536" height="196" fill="#DCE5D6" opacity="0.7"/><text x="590" y="560" font-size="14" fill="#8FA689" text-anchor="end" font-weight="700">easiest to reach</text><rect x="62" y="700" width="536" height="18" fill="#D9B48A"/><rect x="84" y="262" width="126" height="438" rx="4" fill="#FBF8F3" stroke="#B98A5E" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="192" cy="490" r="6" fill="#B98A5E"/><rect x="104" y="300" width="70" height="150" rx="6" fill="#DCE5D6" stroke="#8FA689" stroke-width="2"/><rect x